    desc: 'Three-strip Technicolor (1932-1955) is cinema\'s most committed color system: a beam-splitting camera shot three film records through filters, dyes were imbibition-printed in a dedicated lab, and studio color consultants (the Technicolor "color supervisor" role — Natalie Kalmus above all) forced art direction, costumes and even actor makeup to serve the dye gamut. Results: the poppy fields of The Wizard of Oz, the ink-lagoon of The Adventures of Robin Hood, MGM\'s chorus-line reds. Modern digital "technicolor" grades quote its healthy skin tones and deep primaries; the original dye-transfer prints remain collector icons of depth and stability.',
