    desc: 'Dogme 95 was a manifesto with teeth: von Trier and Vinterberg\'s 1995 "Vow of Chastity" banned artificial light, props, sets, genre music, optical filters and credit-list vanity — shooting only on location with handheld cameras and ambient sound. The Celebration (1998) and The Idiots (1998) proved the aesthetic: grainy, immediate, actor-forward films whose constraints manufactured emotional rawness. Thirty-plus certified Dogme films (Julien Donkey-Boy, Italian for Beginners) followed before the movement wound down in 2005. Its legacy is the normalized digital-naturalism of festival drama — handheld, available-light, imperfect — and a demonstration that constraint breeds style.',
