    desc: 'The high-contrast noir comic — canonized by Frank Miller\'s Sin City — draws with absence: massive black ink masses, few interior lines, spotlight-white faces and the occasional single accent color (the red dress, the yellow bastard). The tradition descends from Silver Streak horror and film-noir stills but was Miller\'s Sin City (1991) that made posterized chiaroscuro a working method: brush-drawn blacks as architecture, hatchless shadows, mood carried entirely by value structure. Eddie Campbell, Sean Phillips (Criminal) and Sean Murphy work the dialect; the style\'s digital descendant is the high-contrast webcomic frame, and its film adaptation (2005) moved the look to screen almost unchanged.',
