    desc: 'The Golden Age of comics (1938-1956) is inseparable from its cheap physical medium: rough newsprint, four-color offset with heavy dot gain, registration drift and editorial reds that bled into hero capes. The look — saturated-but-murky primaries, hand-lettered captions, cover-dated yellowing — was forged by constraint and speed (Will Eisner\'s shop, Jack Kirby & Joe Simon\'s assembly lines), and the surviving artifacts (Action #1, Detective #27, Captain Marvel Adventures) are valued as much for their print decay as their art. Modern "newsprint" reprints and aged-paper digital filters deliberately reproduce the look: golden-age decay is now an aesthetic decision.',
