    desc: 'International Klein Blue (IKB) is less a pigment than an artwork: the matte, velvet-deep ultramarine Yves Klein developed with chemist Edouard Adam (suspended in a synthetic resin binder that preserved raw pigment luminosity) and registered under a Soleau envelope in 1960. Klein showed it as monochrome canvases, sponges, and the living-brush "Anthropométries" — color stripped of composition until the hue itself carries the event. IKB escaped the gallery: design, fashion, album covers (Derek Jarman\'s Blue cinema), and every "Klein blue" sneaker quote it. The aesthetic lesson: total commitment to one color can be maximalism, not minimalism.',
