    desc: 'Pop art\'s silkscreen practice — Andy Warhol\'s Factory line above all — took mass-media imagery (soup cans, Marilyn stills, death-crash photos) and printed it with visible misregistration, repeating grids and flat commercial color. The silkscreen was the point: an industrial reproduction process inside fine art made originality itself the joke. Roy Lichtenstein\'s Benday-dot paintings, Richard Hamilton\'s collages and Paolozzi\'s print pop belong to the same logic; Warhol\'s variations (32 Campbells Soups, Marilyn diptychs) turned multiplication into portraiture. Its graphic legacy is everywhere: editorial flat color, brand-irony design, and the "pop filter" in every image editor.',
