    sum: 'The same color, two ways: Albers proved color only exists in relation.',
    desc: 'Josef Albers built the 20th century\'s color pedagogy around one demonstration: identical colors look different depending on their neighbors. His color courses (Bauhaus, Black Mountain College, Yale) ran students through decades of cut-paper experiments — make one color look like two, make two look like one — and culminated in the 1963 Interaction of Color and the Homage to the Square series (1950-76), a thousand nested squares where inner "glow" is pure perceptual effect. The lessons — simultaneous contrast, vibrating boundaries, transparency illusions — are baked into every design curriculum and palette tool; "Albers exercise" remains a working verb among colorists.',
