    sum: 'The fan-deck as worldview: color standardized, licensed, and traded like stocks.',
    desc: 'Pantone built the 20th century\'s color infrastructure: Lawrence Herbert\'s 1963 ink-matching cards became the Pantone Matching System — numbered swatches (PMS 485 red, 286 blue, 448C "sepiatone") that printers, brands and fashion pipelines treat as legal truth. The fan-deck\'s matte-chip presentation became an aesthetic itself: brands defend trademarked Pantones (Tiffany 1837, UPS brown), the Color of the Year shapes retail seasons, and designers quote the chip-card layout as design shorthand for authority. Pantone\'s systemization also carried costs — standardized sameness debates — but as visual culture the numbered chip is as canonical as the hex code.',
