    sum: 'Cyan shadows, hot skin: the blockbuster grade so common it became a meme.',
    desc: 'The teal-and-orange grade is the digital-intermediate era\'s signature look: skin tones pushed warm-orange against shadows and skies crushed toward teal-cyan, exploiting maximal hue opposition while keeping faces luminous. Born when DI (digital intermediate) suites made per-channel grading trivial after O Brother, Where Art Thou? (2000) proved whole-film color control, the grade became blockbuster default — Transformers, Mad Max: Fury Road, every action key-art — and eventually a design meme ("everything is orange and teal"). Its power is real: complementary contrast plus protected skin reads well on dark screens. Its ubiquity made neutrality feel like rebellion.',
