    desc: 'Haussmann\'s renovation of Paris under Napoleon III sliced medieval districts into the long boulevards and uniform facades that define the city: cream Lutetian limestone, height-limited buildings with rusticated ground floors, continuous wrought-iron balconies aligned by étage, and steep zinc mansard roofs. Beyond aesthetics the program served circulation, hygiene and counter-insurgency; displaced populations paid the human cost, and the "haussmannien" block became both civic icon and political battleground. Its visual legacy is total: every photograph of Paris rooftops, every "Parisian" interior trend (parquet, moulding, tall French windows), and every city-planning argument since.',
