    desc: 'Gilded Age interiors converted industrial fortunes into continental display: Fifth Avenue and Newport mansions (Richard Morris Hunt\'s Breakers) filled with Italian marble, French silk wall panels, Japanese lacquer corners, oak galleries, and rooms purchased — sometimes entire ceilings — from European palaces. Decorators (the Herter Brothers, Ogden Codman with Edith Wharton\'s The Decoration of Houses) professionalized the field; every surface carried currency: parquet inlays, gilded mouldings, tapestry-lined halls, and the double-parlor social machine. The style\'s excess seeded both the American Renaissance and its reform backlash — and remains the visual grammar of "old money" costume drama.',
