    sum: 'Marble courtyards, sunface marquetry and mirror halls — absolutism as décor.',
    desc: 'The Louis XIV style is the visual language of absolutism: Versailles\' marble courtyards, the Hall of Mirrors\' arcade, Le Brun\'s painted apotheoses, Boulle marquetry (brass-and-tortoiseshell inlays), sun-face ornament (the king\'s emblem) and gardens where hedges obey geometry. Colbert\'s royal manufactories (Gobelins) industrialized luxury so French taste could be exported as policy; European courts copied the vocabulary for a century. The style\'s heaviness — massive commodes, gilt consoles, ceremonial ceilings — reads as power arithmetic: symmetry, axis, and the king everywhere implied.',
